1. Retatrutide more than doubled Ozempic=E2=80=99s weight loss in type 2 diab=
etes
What changed
In the 80-week Phase 3 TRANSCEND-T2D-2 trial, retatrutide 12 mg reduced HbA1c=
 by 2.2 percentage points and body weight by 20.9%, versus 1.8 points and 9.4=
% with semaglutide 2 mg. The 9-mg dose produced the same HbA1c reduction and =
19.5% weight loss. Adverse-event discontinuations were 4.8% and 7.9% with ret=
atrutide 9 mg and 12 mg, versus 2.7% with Ozempic. (
Eli Lilly,
Retatrutide delivered greater A1C reduction and more than double the weight l=
oss of Ozempic=E2=80=99s highest approved dose
, October 28, 2026
)
What it tells us
The result strengthens the scientific case that adding glucagon agonism can e=
xtend efficacy beyond single-receptor GLP-1 therapy in diabetes. Commercial d=
ifferentiation is less settled: semaglutide is no longer the strongest compar=
ator in the market, and retatrutide=E2=80=99s higher discontinuation rate may=
 matter in chronic treatment. The trial confirms a large efficacy gap against=
 Ozempic, not superiority to Lilly=E2=80=99s own tirzepatide.
What to watch next
TRIUMPH-5, which compares retatrutide directly with tirzepatide, will determi=
ne whether the triple agonist creates a new premium tier or primarily extends=
 Lilly=E2=80=99s control of the high-efficacy incretin segment.
2. FDA and CMS built a portfolio pathway for individualized genome editing
What changed
FDA=E2=80=99s October 27 draft guidance proposed that multiple genome-editing=
 variants targeting different mutations in one gene could reside within a sin=
gle BLA. Variants falling within a predefined =E2=80=9CIndividualized Feature=
 Space=E2=80=9D could be added after approval using laboratory safety and act=
ivity data rather than new clinical trials. CMS simultaneously announced plan=
s to expand its voluntary Cell and Gene Therapy Access Model beyond sickle ce=
ll disease, using federally negotiated outcomes-based agreements for state Me=
dicaid programs. (
FDA,
Individualized Human Genome Editing Products: Considerations for BLA Submissi=
on and Postapproval Management
, October 2026
;
CMS,
CGT Access Model builds on initial success with new therapies to be added
, October 27, 2026
)
FDA then finalized lifecycle potency guidance on November 2 covering raw-mate=
rial variability, manufacturing controls and biologically relevant batch test=
ing. (
FDA,
FDA provides clarity to support cell and gene therapy development and quality
, November 2, 2026
)
What it tells us
The combined approach addresses two barriers that make mutation-specific ther=
apies uneconomic: repeated product-level clinical development and fragmented =
Medicaid contracting. It favors reusable editing, delivery and manufacturing =
platforms over isolated one-patient programs. The tradeoff is a higher early =
CMC burden, because sponsors must define and validate the boundaries within w=
hich variants remain comparable.
What to watch next
Whether final guidance preserves the no-new-trial pathway, which diseases CMS=
 selects, and whether manufacturers accept outcomes-based pricing for therapi=
es with limited long-term evidence.
